# Render performance.  Baseline on the idle desktop was 3 FPS / refr 256 ms
# (render 230 ms + flush 22 ms) / CPU 94%, i.e. 91% of the time is software
# pixel compositing, not panel transfer.  Three changes attack that:
#
# 1. PARTIAL instead of one screen-sized buffer.  With a single full-screen
#    buffer LVGL sets RENDER_MODE_FULL, and lv_refr.c rewrites *any*
#    invalidation -- one character in the clock -- into the whole 390x450
#    area.  A 60-row partial buffer is 390*60*2 = 46,800 B, which fits the
#    ~65 KB of free SRAM heap, so it also stops the draw buffer living in
#    QSPI PSRAM.  Verified safe on this driver: LVGL reshapes the buffer
#    stride per sub-area, and lcd_dev.c derives row_size = cols * pixel_size
#    when lcddev_area_s.stride is 0, which matches.
# 2. -O2 for the LVGL library only.  CONFIG_LV_OPTLEVEL is applied with
#    target_compile_options(lvgl PRIVATE ...), so it does not touch the
#    Bluetooth stack or anything whose .bss layout the latent heap-corruption
#    bug is sensitive to.
# 3. The ARMv8-M assembly memcpy.  LV_USE_CLIB_STRING=y sends every bulk
#    pixel move to libc memcpy, and the only implementation built today is
#    the byte/word-at-a-time C one in lib_bsdmemcpy.c compiled at -O0.
# CONFIG_LV_NUTTX_LCD_SINGLE_BUFFER is not set
# EXPERIMENT REVERTED (2026-10-16).  Two things were learned and both are
# worth keeping in mind:
#   1. The buffer really is NOT in SRAM.  lv_malloc() placed it at 0x6007ff60
#      = PSRAM, because this board merges the SRAM and PSRAM heaps into one
#      arena (CONFIG_MM_REGIONS=2, no CONFIG_MM_KERNEL_HEAP), so the "fits the
#      ~65 KB of free SRAM heap" reasoning above no longer holds -- making the
#      buffer static overflows the sram region by 29,632 B, i.e. only ~28 KB
#      is actually free now.
#   2. ...but that does NOT matter.  Pinning the buffer into SRAM (static
#      array, 30 rows to fit) left the scroll cost unchanged:
#          PSRAM 60 rows: render 74-115 ms, flush 23-34 ms, CPU 87-89%, 5-8 FPS
#          SRAM  30 rows: render 70-105 ms, flush 31-44 ms, CPU 88-90%, 4-7 FPS
#      Render is identical and flush got WORSE (30 rows = twice as many bands).
#      So the scroll cost is raw CPU pixel work in LVGL's software renderer,
#      not memory bandwidth.  Reverted to 60 rows.
#
# NOTE: CONFIG_LV_NUTTX_LCD_CUSTOM_BUFFER=y is what selects the partial-buffer
# path.  It MUST stay -- with none of the three choice symbols set, Kconfig
# falls back to the first one (SINGLE_BUFFER), which silently switches the
# display to RENDER_MODE_FULL and makes every tiny invalidation repaint the
# whole 390x450 area.  (Lost it once during an edit on 2026-10-16 and the
# config came back as BUFFER_COUNT=1.)
CONFIG_LV_NUTTX_LCD_CUSTOM_BUFFER=y
CONFIG_LV_NUTTX_LCD_BUFFER_SIZE=60
# -O2 for the lvgl target only, applied by
# target_compile_options(lvgl PRIVATE ${CONFIG_LV_OPTLEVEL}).
#
# Two things to know before changing this value:
#  - It must stay a single flag.  apps/graphics/lvgl/CMakeLists.txt:187 writes
#    if(NOT ${CONFIG_LV_OPTLEVEL} STREQUAL "") with the variable unquoted, so a
#    semicolon-separated list turns into extra if() arguments and CMake fails;
#    a space-separated string survives the if() but is then handed to gcc as one
#    argv entry and gcc rejects it.
#  - NuttX adds -fno-strict-aliasing only when the *global* level leaves
#    DEBUG_NOOPT (arch/arm/src/cmake/gcc.cmake:116), and the global level stays
#    at NOOPT here, so LVGL gets -O2 with strict aliasing live.  That is how
#    LVGL upstream is normally built, and Round 23 confirmed the touch fault
#    was below LVGL, not caused by this -- but it is the first thing to undo if
#    a rendering oddity ever appears.
CONFIG_LV_OPTLEVEL="-O2"
